    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d328">

                <pb facs="0756" n="730" xml:id="pg730"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">788.11/5–2053: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 328<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Embassy in
                        Iran</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d328-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top
                            secret<lb/>priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1953-05-28T17:51:00-04:00">May 28, 1953—5:51
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3067. British Embassy Washington has received following message from Churchill reurtel 4474:<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d328-f2"/>
                    <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“You may certainly inform the State Department that while we do not
                            interfere in Persian politics we should be very sorry to see the Shah
                            lose his powers or leave his post or be driven out. Perhaps Mr.
                                Henderson will convey this
                            assurance to the Shah and say that it comes personally from
                        me.”</p></quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Smith</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d328-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d328-f1-content">
                <p>Drafted and signed by Richards.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d328-f2" n="2" xml:id="d328-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 4474, May 20, Ambassador Henderson informed the Department that he spoke with an emissary
                    of the Shah on May 17, who said that the Shah was disturbed by the British
                    attitude toward him and wanted a clarifying statement from Henderson as to the British attitude before
                        Henderson departed for Washington
                    early in June. (788.11/5–2053)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
